<h2 id="philly-taxes">Pennsylvania and Philadelphia taxes for side hustlers</h2>
<p>This is the section most side hustle guides skip — and the one that matters most in Philly. The city taxes self-employed income in ways most other cities don't.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pennsylvania income tax:</strong> a flat 3.07% on taxable income, including side hustle profit.</li>
  <li><strong>Wage Tax vs. Net Profits Tax:</strong> your W-2 job withholds the city Wage Tax (3.735% for residents from July 1, 2026). Self-employment profit instead falls under the Net Profits Tax, which was 3.74% for residents and 3.43% for non-residents for tax year 2025. Rates have been stepping down slowly, so check the current figure when you file.</li>
  <li><strong>BIRT:</strong> the Business Income and Receipts Tax applies to anyone doing business in the city and has two parts — a small tax on gross receipts (1.41 mills, or $1.41 per $1,000, for 2025) and a tax on net income (5.71% for 2025). Starting with tax year 2025, the old exemption for the first $100,000 of receipts no longer applies, so even small sole proprietors generally must file. Part of the BIRT net income portion can be credited against your Net Profits Tax.</li>
  <li><strong>Commercial Activity License:</strong> required for for-profit activity in the city. Note that holding one means you file BIRT every year, even if you report no business.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> 8% in Philadelphia (6% state plus 2% local) on most physical goods. Register with the state if you sell products; most personal services aren't taxed.</li>
  <li><strong>Federal:</strong> 15.3% self-employment tax plus income tax on net profit. The <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> helps you estimate the set-aside.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> City tax rules have changed several times recently, and proposals to ease BIRT for sole proprietors have been debated. Check the Philadelphia Department of Revenue site each year or use a local preparer — it usually pays for itself in your first season.</div>

